{
  _id: ObjectId('684ad64bcb84beee85782e23'),
  user_id: 'c59957fdd1ae1f83b076c333215597d3',
  book_id: '7235532',
  review_id: '74e332ea6902c1810f6dfd547606ece1',
  rating: 4,
  review_text: "I don't usually read ginormous epic fantasy series. Trilogies are about as much as I can take. I have occasionally read the first book in a long series for group reads, but I rarely read more than the first volume. I generally have no desire to spend 10,000+ pages in one universe, especially when the series isn't even finished. However, I have really enjoyed the books I've read by Brandon Sanderson, and I know that he's really good at getting books done in a timely manner. So, I decided to listen to the audiobook of The Way of Kings, the first of a planned 10-book series. In print, this book is over 1000 pages, so the series is everything I try to avoid. \n" +
    " I found The Way of Kings to be quite engaging and entertaining. Despite it's length and scope, I never got lost or confused. I did find that some parts did drag and that the book strayed for too long in parts from the storylines that I was most interested in. However, it was all good. I really can't wait for the next installment. \n" +
    " The audio production was very good as well. I would have liked more of Kate Reading's parts, but not enough of the story stuck with the characters she was reading. I really think audio is the way to go with a book of this size. It keeps you on track. At 45+ hours, I'd definitely say I got my Audible credit's worth.",
  date_added: 'Fri Sep 17 16:14:36 -0700 2010',
  date_updated: 'Sat Oct 30 10:10:10 -0700 2010',
  read_at: 'Wed Oct 20 00:00:00 -0700 2010',
  started_at: 'Fri Sep 17 00:00:00 -0700 2010',
  n_votes: 4,
  n_comments: 0
}
{
  _id: ObjectId('684ad5e7cb84beee851d2a35'),
  user_id: 'f070f1cc66d79485f12f9a185afb9413',
  book_id: '21407444',
  review_id: '2d94d5cdd1448b4cc9b6c60abcd74407',
  rating: 0,
  review_text: 'http://openbooksociety.com/article/bo... \n' +
    ' By OBS reviewer Una \n' +
    " I thoroughly enjoyed this story set in the last years of the Second World War. Eileen is an 11 year old who doesn't want to work in the fields so lies about her age to get a domestic position in Mrs. Mumson's boarding house. Mrs. Mumson is a character who speaks to her dead husband's picture and even sets a place for him at the table. Even as an 11 year old Eileen can see all the things that need to be done to get the house in order. Eileen certainly possesses a maturity that not many children would have today. She is the maid and responsible for all the housework as well as the cook. It is really humorous how she develops war time recipes from the radio program she listens to. Some of the meals go over well and others do not get great reviews but all keep within the budget of food stamps and rations. The six characters living in the house basically show society of the time with all its problems, and intolerance of a country reeling from the effects of war. \n" +
    ` The Doctor who is dealing with a polio outbreak which has produced fear among the majority of the population and the rampant spread of communicable diseases is the first to realize that they are working Eileen too hard and that it might have consequences. An unplanned pregnancy is suspected and the "father" also a boarding house guest doesn't want to own up to it, leaving the girl to deal with the consequences of possibly losing her job and also her place at the boarding house. When one of Japanese boys who worked for Mrs. Mumson prior to the evacuation shows up unannounced he is met with hatred and intolerance by one of the guests. The book really shows the turbulence of the times. \n` +
    ' Eileen sees and hears all of this however her shining star is Teddy who seems to have a secret life and no one knows for sure whether it is legitimate work that he does. Eileen only sees that he does nice things for her and his best asset is that he always is driving a new car. She knows that Teddy will stick up for her no matter what the problem. \n' +
    ' The book follows Eileen during the 4 months she spends at the Boarding House and sees her start to grow up and have her first love. Boarding House Stew is a well written true story and I know I would love to be able to see the play version.',
  date_added: 'Tue Jul 28 19:05:46 -0700 2015',
  date_updated: 'Fri Jul 31 17:57:12 -0700 2015',
  read_at: 'Fri Jul 31 00:00:00 -0700 2015',
  started_at: 'Tue Jul 28 00:00:00 -0700 2015',
  n_votes: 0,
  n_comments: 0
}
{
  _id: ObjectId('684ad5b2cb84beee85ed48c0'),
  user_id: 'd5648a8d0b356041fd146dbf6c020ebd',
  book_id: '17573615',
  review_id: 'b2c323e9077effe03271710d08c372ce',
  rating: 4,
  review_text: 'This was a great read about the planning of a wedding in Ireland. Cara and Shane receive an invitation to a lavish wedding and by chance chatting to the bridegroom they discover how unhappy he is with all the extravagence and that they are in debt because of it. They decide that when they get married they want a nice quiet wedding with just their closest family and friends. \n' +
    " However when they announce their holiday plans- both mothers turn into diva's- wanting to take over and organise one of the biggest weddings Ireland has ever seen! \n" +
    ' They make their plans despite family opposition and it causes a lot of friction in every camp. There are lots of secrets being kept and astonishing proposals made. \n' +
    ' I was rooting for them to get the perfect wedding that they wanted.',
  date_added: 'Thu Jul 18 06:05:03 -0700 2013',
  date_updated: 'Fri Jul 19 23:35:14 -0700 2013',
  read_at: 'Fri Jul 19 00:00:00 -0700 2013',
