{
  _id: ObjectId('684ad648cb84beee85751057'),
  user_id: 'f6a177eb9de2ea8bcd2e9798072dbc60',
  book_id: '16138688',
  review_id: '4a6f79d3460db75f27f246d0432b67d2',
  rating: 3,
  review_text: "The One Book One Brant event is taking place today and this year's book choice was The Painted Girls by Canadian author Cathy Marie Buchanan. My second reading of this novel only reinforced how fortunate it is to born at a time when women have ample options to look after themselves and their families independently. \n" +
    ' This piece of historical fiction shares the tale of a poor family who struggle to pay the rent for their room after their father dies. Their mother cannot cope with her life and lives in a haze of absinthe. When she is able, she works at a laundry and otherwise leaves her daughters to look after each other. The three girls must contribute, even the youngest earning francs dancing as a "petit rat" at the Opera House. \n' +
    ' The story focuses on the lives of the two oldest sisters. Antoinette, the oldest daughter is no longer able to dance in the ballet and unfortunately falls in love with an unseemly boy, Emile. As she tries to care for her sisters, she spends less and less time at home while she is "adored" by Emile. She sees the best in him while her sister, Marie worries about their connection and her fears are realized when Emile is jailed for a brutal crime. Antoinette is forced to learn about the true nature of her lover the hard way, spending time imprisoned with nuns but learning a new skill for her release. \n' +
    ' Marie, the middle daughter, dances for the ballet and attracts the attention of the artist Edgar Degas, who pays her to model for him each week. He sketches, paints and ultimately completes a wax statue of her in 1881 which received much criticism. Marie has always seen herself as ugly and this is reinforced by the reviews which are cruelly read to her by her "protector", a man who has taken advantage of a young girl who needed money to support her ballet lessons and her family. \n' +
    ' During my first read of this novel, I had not realized that the girls were based on real individuals that lived in France. This made me curious to view the actual statue named Little Dancer Age Fourteen which was central to the novel. I look forward to meeting Buchanan and gaining addition insight into this novel this afternoon. \n' +
    ' http://ayearofbooksblog.com/2016/02/2...',
  date_added: 'Sun Feb 28 07:05:07 -0800 2016',
  date_updated: 'Fri May 20 14:56:23 -0700 2016',
  read_at: 'Sun Feb 28 07:05:43 -0800 2016',
  started_at: '',
  n_votes: 1,
  n_comments: 0
}
{
  _id: ObjectId('684ad5cacb84beee8502fdfb'),
  user_id: '7a576f521f185c22e02dda3a9b1fc97c',
  book_id: '13324841',
  review_id: '94595ac9d1e3985baf1a6bcaceef2b4e',
  rating: 3,
  review_text: 'This was definitely the weakest of the Giver Quartet books. It follows the story of Claire, the Birthmother of Gabriel, and her journey from the town that they grew up in to try and find her son. \n' +
    " I loved her story in the town, how different she was because she never took the pills for the Stirrings, no Birthmothers are allowed to. So Claire felt love, and when she gave birth - she felt love for her son, and she didn't want to lose that connection. Through a somewhat unbelievable journey (after all, when Jonas stole an apple after lunch at school, the people of the town knew), Claire is able to form a bond with her son. When Jonas takes Gabriel away, Claire escapes on a boat to try and catch up with him. She is shipwrecked and loses her memory. \n" +
    ' The town whose shore Claire washes up on takes to her immediately. As she regains her memory of her son, though, she realizes she must leave. Take us through a Rocky montage of her training, and she climbs a cliff in order to leave the town. On the top of the cliff, we meet the Trademaster who we remember from the previous book. Trademaster takes her youth, so Claire gets to see her son, but as an old woman instead of the young, vibrant woman she was. \n' +
    " It's a good wrap up book, I guess. The loose ends (except for exactly what happened in the town after Jonas left and the people of the town who gained back things like color) were neatly wrapped up and any questions that were left from previous books, for the most part, were tied up. \n" +
    ' I just feel a little unsatisfied.',
  date_added: 'Tue Sep 24 06:11:24 -0700 2013',
  date_updated: 'Tue Oct 22 10:40:44 -0700 2013',
  read_at: 'Tue Oct 22 10:40:44 -0700 2013',
  started_at: 'Tue Oct 15 00:00:00 -0700 2013',
  n_votes: 0,
  n_comments: 0
}
